WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.488 --> 00:00:02.331
(birds chirping)

00:00:02.331 --> 00:00:04.610
(mosquito buzzing)

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:07.630
- The Dismal Swamp is an environment

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:11.113
that has hazardous conditions
associated with it.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.490
The heat.

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:16.440
You got poisonous snakes
and mosquitoes come out

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.443
at certain times of the
year and it's unbearable.

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:23.030
But when you think about some

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:27.043
of the torture the
enslaved peoples endured,

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.480
I think if most of us were
living during that day

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.690
and we had an option to go
in the swamp and live freely,

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:37.788
I think a lot of us
would take that option.

00:00:37.788 --> 00:00:40.538
(birds chirping)

00:00:47.762 --> 00:00:49.707
(dramatic music)

00:00:49.707 --> 00:00:52.200
- [Yvonne] From the 17th
century until the Civil War,

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.370
escaped slaves, known
as maroons, found refuge

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:56.883
in the Great Dismal Swamp.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:01.420
Located on the border of
Virginia and North Carolina,

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:04.760
the swamp was wild and inhospitable.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:06.390
Nevertheless, it was there

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:09.530
that runaways created
self-sufficient communities

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:12.313
almost entirely cut off
from the outside world.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.040
For the past 10 years,

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:19.040
archaeologist Dan Sayers
from American University

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.270
has been studying these settlements.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.520
- Part of what I did was develop

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.130
this sort of research program called

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:27.720
the Great Dismal Swamp Landscape Study.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.710
Most of my focus has been
on archeological discovery

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:34.410
of sites out in the swamp as
well as historical research

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.220
and then, of course,
trying to understand it.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:38.500
Before we started our research,

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:40.260
there was a sense people
lived in the swamp

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.410
for a variety of reasons
but we really wanted

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:44.780
to go in the swamp and find remnants

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:46.260
of their lives out there.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.380
And sure enough, we've been able to look

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:50.540
at several sites associated with several

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:51.840
of these different groups.

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:59.470
Probably an 1790 to 1825
or '30 nail that we found

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:02.890
in one of the resistance
community settlements.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:05.370
I'm sure the population went up and down

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:08.190
but very strong potential
for any given moment

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:10.890
for thousands of people that
went out into the Great Dismal

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:14.500
and formed these
multi-generational communities.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.313
It was a radical form of
resistance in a good way.

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:21.230
- [Yvonne] The runaways
who escaped slavery

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:23.640
risked everything to be free.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:24.810
Even if it meant living

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.710
in the harsh environment of the swamp.

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.100
One former slave who briefly lived

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.223
in the swamp was a man named Moses Grandy.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:36.730
- I've done family genealogy research

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:41.730
in which I've uncovered a slave
narrative by Moses Grandy,

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:45.993
who I believe is my great
great grandfather's uncle.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:48.930
This is Edmond Grandy,

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:51.190
my great great grandfather's headstone,

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:53.563
who was born in 1827.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.430
He was a waterman and a boatsman
on the Dismal Swamp Canal,

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:00.380
which was being hand dug.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.440
And he describes very vividly

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:05.090
in the slave narrative
how they were treated,

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:07.883
what they endured, how they were tortured.

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:11.840
If you had that kind of experience

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:15.240
and you had an opportunity
to come out to the swamp

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:20.220
and live freely under
challenging conditions,

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:23.020
you're gonna have a hard time
getting them out of there.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:29.360
The idea of Africans
setting up communities

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:34.360
and being in charge of
their destiny in the swamp,

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.603
I don't want people to
ever forget this history.

